win32u: the nonclient trace names the window rect as well.

The question this trace is now asked is which of the two rectangles is wrong.
A ring of unpainted pixels around a window means the client is smaller than the
window, and whether Wine reserved that or the program claimed it by answering
WM_NCCALCSIZE itself is not answerable from the client rect alone -- which is
exactly the ambiguity that made a measured 11 pixel inset unattributable after
0065 removed every inset Wine applies.

--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -1820,8 +1820,19 @@
      * line per non-client paint of a visible window and it names, in the units
      * the bug is argued in, exactly which pixels the frame agrees not to
      * touch. WINEDEBUG=+nonclient prints it. */
-    TRACE_(nonclient)( "hwnd %p exclude client %s (screen)\n", hwnd,
-                       wine_dbgstr_rect( &rectClient ) );
+    {
+        /* Vessel: the window rect beside the client rect, because the question
+         * this trace is now asked is which of the two is wrong. A ring of
+         * unpainted pixels means the client is smaller than the window, and
+         * whether Wine reserved that or the program claimed it in
+         * WM_NCCALCSIZE is not answerable from the client rect alone. */
+        RECT rectWindow;
+
+        get_window_rect_rel( hwnd, COORDS_SCREEN, &rectWindow, get_thread_dpi() );
+        TRACE_(nonclient)( "hwnd %p window %s client %s (screen)\n", hwnd,
+                           wine_dbgstr_rect( &rectWindow ),
+                           wine_dbgstr_rect( &rectClient ) );
+    }
 
     if (clip > (HRGN)1)
     {
